{"kind":"artifact","text":"Read the repository Git was pointed at, not the one the environment names. Exact clean pushed head 905e0b059ec419ab6e60b2ab161dc6169f932353 on request/repo-remote-link; tree f08ef41f, checkout clean, origin matching, base equals current main c679fb73. Under planner's repair child 3b06d237 and my promise 22a0df9c.\n\nTHE DEFECT I SHIPPED AND CODEX CAUGHT. I made `git config --local` the fix for reading only this repository's remotes, and published a contract saying the read is repository-local. --local bounds which configuration file is consulted. It does not bound which repository Git resolves. With GIT_COMMON_DIR pointing elsewhere, the exact command returned another repository's remote, exit 0. Every downstream defence — the allowlists, the bounds, the browser's refusal — sat downstream of a value that could already have come from somewhere else.\n\nWIDER THAN THE VERDICT SAID. Codex named the remote read. Five call sites in this package inherit the environment, and all five are redirectable: the config read, `worktree list`, `rev-parse --show-toplevel`, the per-worktree `git status` that decides clean or dirty, and the `git grep` behind citingDocuments. GIT_WORK_TREE makes the served path itself report the attacker's directory. GIT_INDEX_FILE makes a clean checkout report \"unavailable\".\n\nONE OF THEM IS A GUARD BYPASS, NOT A DISPLAY DEFECT. GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS and GIT_NOGLOB_PATHSPECS turn citingDocuments' `*.md` pathspec into a literal filename matching nothing. The citation gate then returns an empty answer, and an empty answer is what lets a cited retirement land. So the pathspec family is stripped alongside the routing family, and the comment says why.\n\nTHE FIX. gitRoutingVariables and repositoryLocalGit, one constructor, applied at all five sites. The only remaining bare git invocation in this package is inside the constructor itself.\n\nDELIBERATELY NOT STRIPPED, because it looks like an omission and is not. GIT_CONFIG and GIT_CONFIG_* stay. internal/gitstore and internal/testgit pin the system and global config to /dev/null through exactly that family, and safe.directory — which decides whether git will read a repository owned by another user at all — normally lives in the scope they point at. Stripping them would remove protection rather than add it. I measured that core.worktree injected through GIT_CONFIG_COUNT does not redirect `rev-parse --show-toplevel` or `worktree list`, so the config family is not a routing vector here.\n\nTHE SECOND BLOCKER. webRemoteURL now tests the raw string for \"?\" and \"#\" before net/url.Parse sees it, and the post-parse check is gone rather than kept beside it. Go's parser drops a bare trailing \"#\" and re-serializes without it, so the old check admitted \"https://host/repo.git#\" while the browser preserved the delimiter and refused. Checking raw bytes is both the fix and the simplification.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, run and watched, tree restored clean after each. Reverting the config call site alone fails TestLocalWorktreesIgnoresInheritedGitRoutingVariables/{GIT_DIR,GIT_COMMON_DIR} and the matching service subtests. Reverting the grep site fails TestCitingDocumentsIgnoresInheritedGitRoutingVariables/{GIT_LITERAL_PATHSPECS,GIT_NOGLOB_PATHSPECS,GIT_DIR} with \"hid the citing page: got [], want [page.md]\". Reverting webRemoteURL to the post-parse check fails TestWebRemoteURLAdmitsOnlyHTTPAndHTTPS by name.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT TAKE. internal/gitstore is redirectable the same way — cat-file returns another repository's object through a pinned --git-dir — and app.Open itself resolves through it. That is a kernel-boundary change this row does not authorize; it is planner's separate request d2605fd7, gated on a threat-model ruling from hugh. The regressions here open the workspace before setting the variable so they isolate these five sites, and the comments say so.\n\nPROVENANCE NOTE. This rests on my promise 22a0df9c, which merge 6bb01a86 world-staled while the work was in flight. Nothing about the authorization changed — the promise is live, not retired — so I signed the dead-basis override rather than manufacture a fresh commitment. That refusal treating merely-stale as dead is the defect planner refiled at 769006ed.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit — not the pre-recut base and not the implementing agent0s numbers. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures, zero cached — cmd/gs 262.337s, internal/app 165.441s, internal/kernel 164.733s, cmd/gitseq-mcp 103.609s, internal/docset 103.523s, internal/service 60.676s. npm test 131 pass, 0 fail. gofmt -l . empty; go build and go vet exit 0; git diff --check exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0.","body":{"commit":"905e0b059ec419ab6e60b2ab161dc6169f932353","dead_basis_override":"true","path":"internal/app"}}